'''Kimberly Kay Reynolds''' (née '''Strawn'''; born August 4, 1959) is an American politician serving since 2017 as the 43rd [[List of governors of Iowa|governor of Iowa]]. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], she is the first [[List of female governors in the United States|female governor]] in Iowa history.

Reynolds was elected [[Clarke County, Iowa|Clarke County]] [[treasurer]] in 1994 and served for four terms in that office. She then served a partial term in the [[Iowa Senate]] from 2009 to 2011. From 2011 to 2017, Reynolds served as the 46th [[List of lieutenant governors of Iowa|lieutenant governor of Iowa]]. She became governor in May 2017 when Governor [[Terry Branstad]] stepped down to become the [[List of ambassadors of the United States to China|United States ambassador to China]]. Reynolds won a full term as governor in [[2018 Iowa gubernatorial election|2018]] and was reelected in [[2022 Iowa gubernatorial election|2022]].

Reynolds has signed legislation providing for educational vouchers as well as legislation supporting voting rights for felons and Second Amendment rights. Reynolds received poor approval ratings in 2020 for her handling of the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Iowa|COVID-19 pandemic]]. She has a close relationship with the Iowa [[Pig farming|pork industry]]. Reynolds delivered the Republican response to President [[Joe Biden]]'s [[2022 State of the Union Address]]. In 2023, she signed a six-week [[Abortion in Iowa|abortion ban into law]], and in 2025, she signed a bill repealing anti-discrimination protections for [[LGBTQ rights in Iowa|transgender people]], making Iowa the first state to remove anti-discrimination protections for any group of people.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/02/28/us/iowa-governor-gender-identity-civil-rights-code/index.html|title=Iowa becomes first US state to remove gender identity protections from its civil rights code|agency=Associated Press|date=Feb 28, 2025|website=CNN}}</ref>

On April 11, 2025, Reynolds announced she will not seek reelection in [[2026 Iowa gubernatorial election|2026]].

==Early life and education==
Reynolds was born Kimberly Kay Strawn in 1959 in [[St. Charles, Iowa]]. She graduated from high school at the [[Interstate 35 Community School District]] in 1977.<ref>{{cite news|url=  http://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2017/01/11/kim-reynolds-history-in-iowa/96182144/|title=How Kim Reynolds ascended to Iowa's governorship|newspaper=Des Moines Register|last=Noble|first=Jason|date=January 12, 2017|access-date=April 25, 2019}}</ref>

Reynolds attended [[Northwest Missouri State University]], taking classes in business, consumer sciences and clothing sales and design, without earning a degree. She later took classes at [[Southeastern Community College (Iowa)|Southeastern Community College]] in the late 1980s, and then accounting classes at [[Southwestern Community College (Iowa)|Southwestern Community College]] between 1992 and 1995.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://carrollspaper.com/Content/Opinion-Archive/Opinion/Article/Kim-Reynolds-high-school-graduate-looking-for-work/4/4/10931,|title=Kim Reynolds high school graduate looking for work - Daily Times Herald|website=carrollspaper.com|access-date=June 29, 2017|archive-date=September 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180929011557/http://carrollspaper.com/Content/Opinion-Archive/Opinion/Article/Kim-Reynolds-high-school-graduate-looking-for-work/4/4/10931,|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In 2012, Reynolds began taking classes in the bachelor of public administration program at [[Upper Iowa University]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kcci.com/article/lt-governor-by-day-student-by-night/6875275#|title=Lt. Governor by day, student by night|website=KCCI.com|date=December 25, 2012 }}</ref> 

In December 2016, shortly before Reynolds became governor, [[Iowa State University]] awarded her a [[Bachelor of Liberal Studies]] degree with three concentrations: political science, business management, and communications.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/education/2016/12/17/lt-gov-kim-reynolds-graduates-iowa-state/95568032/ |title=Lt. Gov. Kim Reynolds graduates from Iowa State |newspaper=Des Moines Register |last=Longman |first=Molly |date=December 17, 2016 |access-date=March 31, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://omaha.com/news/state_and_regional/kim-reynolds-path-to-the-iowa-governor-s-office-was-anything-but-typical/article_c2ecf2ca-21fc-5562-8b14-5f780ea0f146.html|title=Kim Reynolds' path to the Iowa Governor's Office was anything but typical|first=Robynn |last=Tysver|website=Omaha.com|date=January 16, 2017 }}</ref>

==Early political career==
[[File:Kim Reynolds - Official Portrait - 83rd GA.jpg|Reynolds during her time in the Iowa Senate|thumb|right]]
Reynolds was elected [[Clarke County, Iowa|Clarke County]] treasurer in 1994 and served four terms.<ref>{{cite web |title=Who is Kim Reynolds? What to know about the Iowa governor giving the State of the Union response |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2022/02/22/who-is-kim-reynolds-iowa-governor-republican-state-of-the-union-2022-response-joe-biden/6893632001/ |website=Des Moines Register |access-date=13 August 2023}}</ref> While she was treasurer she was selected to join the American Council of Young Political Leaders on a trip to [[Taipei, Taiwan]].<ref>{{citeweb|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-des-moines-register-taipei/178385074/|title=Clarke Treasurer chosen for trip to Taipei, Taiwan|date=November 26, 1995|publisher= [[Des Moines Register]]|access-date=August 7, 2025}}</ref>

On November 4, 2008, Reynolds was elected to represent the 48th district in the [[Iowa Senate]], defeating [[Democratic Party (US)|Democratic]] nominee Ruth Smith and independent candidate Rodney Schmidt.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sos.iowa.gov/elections/pdf/2008/OfficialCanvass2008General.pdf|title=State of Iowa Official Canvass Summary: November 4, 2008 General Election|publisher=Office of Iowa Secretary of State}}</ref> In 2010, Reynolds endorsed a ban on [[same-sex marriage in Iowa]].<ref name=":12">{{Cite news|url=https://wcfcourier.com/news/local/lt-governor-candidate-kim-reynolds-endorses-gay-marriage-ban-in/article_11d0c982-a2a8-11df-b6c3-001cc4c03286.html|title=Lt. governor candidate Kim Reynolds endorses gay marriage ban in Waterloo speech|author=Josh Nelson|date=August 7, 2018|work=Waterloo Cedar Falls Courier}}</ref>

==Lieutenant governor of Iowa (2011–2017)==
On June 25, 2010, Republican gubernatorial nominee [[Terry Branstad]] publicly selected Reynolds to be his [[running mate]] as the [[List of lieutenant governors of Iowa|lieutenant governor]] candidate. The next day, she received the Republican nomination at the Republican state convention. On November 2, 2010, the Branstad/Reynolds ticket won the general election.<ref name="auto">{{cite web|url=https://sos.iowa.gov/elections/pdf/2010/govorr.pdf|title=Official results: 2010 General Election held Tuesday, November 2nd 2010|publisher=Iowa Secretary of State's Office}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://wcfcourier.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/terry-branstad-ready-to-lead-the-charge-as-iowa-s/article_ab3d3d26-e6ff-11df-a491-001cc4c03286.html|title=Terry Branstad 'ready to lead the charge' as Iowa's governor|author=Rod Boshart|website=Waterloo Cedar Falls Courier|date=November 3, 2010 }}</ref> Reynolds resigned her Senate seat on November 12 before taking office as lieutenant governor.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.crestonnewsadvertiser.com/articles/2010/11/15/r_eqwbogdws7a3bbc3zhr6uw/index.xml|title=Reynolds resigns, Culver to set special election|last=Wilson|first=Kyle|date=November 15, 2010|access-date=November 16, 2010|newspaper=Creston News Advertiser|publisher=Shaw Media|archive-date=July 8, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110708205915/http://www.crestonnewsadvertiser.com/articles/2010/11/15/r_eqwbogdws7a3bbc3zhr6uw/index.xml|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Reynolds was sworn in as lieutenant governor of Iowa on January 14, 2011.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://awpc.cattcenter.iastate.edu/2017/03/21/inaugural-address-jan-14-2011/|title=Lt. Governor Inaugural Address: Kim Reynolds|date=January 14, 2011|location=Des Moines, Iowa|via=Carrie Chapman Catt Center for Women and Politics, Iowa State University}}</ref> She co-chaired the Governor's Science, Technology, Engineering and Math (STEM) Advisory Council, Iowa Partnership for Economic Progress board, and the Military Children Education Coalition. She was also Branstad's representative on the board of the [[Iowa State Fair]].<ref name="winninggov">{{cite web|url=http://www.governing.com/blogs/politics/gov-successful-lieutenant-governors-seeking-governorship.html|title=How Successful Are Lieutenant Governors Seeking the Governorship?|website=governing.com|access-date=June 29, 2017}}</ref>

Reynolds was elected chair of the [[National Lieutenant Governors Association]] (NLGA) in July 2015.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.theiowastatesman.com/4273/reynolds-named-chair-of-nlga |work=The Iowa Statesman|title=Reynolds named chair of NLGA |date=July 10, 2015|access-date=August 23, 2018}}</ref>

==Governor of Iowa (2017–present)==
On May 24, 2017, Reynolds became governor of Iowa upon the resignation of Branstad, who stepped down to become [[List of ambassadors of the United States to China|United States Ambassador to China]]. She is Iowa's first female governor.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation-politics/kim-reynolds-sworn-in-as-iowas-1st-female-governor |title=Kim Reynolds sworn in as Iowa's 1st female governor |work=Seattle Times |last1= Rodriguez |first1= Barbara |last2= Beaumont |first2= Thomas |date= May 24, 2017 |access-date= August 23, 2018}}</ref>

===Elections===
====2018====
{{Main|2018 Iowa gubernatorial election}}
In June 2017, Reynolds said she would seek a full term as governor of Iowa in the 2018 election.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/2017/06/15/kim-reynolds-acknowledges-plan-seek-full-term-2018/400894001|title=Kim Reynolds acknowledges plan to seek full term in 2018|website=[[The Des Moines Register]] }}</ref>

Reynolds's decision to have Representative [[Steve King]] co-chair her campaign stirred controversy, as King has a history of remarks that have been described as racist.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://eu.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2017/12/12/steve-king-remain-iowa-gov-reynolds-campaign-co-chair/944721001/|title=Iowa Gov. Reynolds will keep Steve King as campaign co-chair|work=Des Moines Register|access-date=November 3, 2018|language=en}}</ref> ''The Des Moines Register'' editorial board wrote, "Gov. Kim Reynolds has kept him on as her campaign co-chairman, while muttering increasingly thin-lipped denials that she agrees with his ideological extremism."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://eu.desmoinesregister.com/story/opinion/editorials/2018/10/31/gop-must-not-shrug-white-nationalist-rhetoric-steve-king-trump/1824574002/|title=GOP can't keep shrugging as Rep. Steve King, President Trump pander to white nationalists|work=Des Moines Register|access-date=November 3, 2018|language=en}}</ref> Reynolds had previously praised King, saying he was "a strong defender of freedom and our conservative values".<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://qctimes.com/news/local/government-and-politics/reynolds-rebuts-hubbell-s-call-to-reproach-u-s-rep/article_e662ed0c-6aad-5963-ac4d-d22ecf25b050.html|title=Reynolds rebuts Hubbell's call to reproach U.S. Rep. Steve King|last=Boshart|first=Rod|work=The Quad-City Times|access-date=November 3, 2018|language=en}}</ref> After Election Day, Reynolds criticized King and said that he needed to change his approach.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://eu.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2018/11/13/iowa-gov-kim-reynolds-steve-king-campaign-house-election-democrats-iowa-politics-republican-scholten/1988933002/|title=Iowa Gov. Kim Reynolds bluntly tells Steve King to decide future amid racism allegations|work=Des Moines Register|access-date=November 14, 2018|language=en}}</ref>

Reynolds won the Republican nomination for governor and defeated [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]] [[Fred Hubbell]] and [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian]] Jake Porter in the general election, 50% to 48%.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://whotv.com/2018/11/07/reynolds-prevails-against-hubbell-in-tight-gubernatorial-race/|title=Reynolds Prevails Against Hubbell in Tight Gubernatorial Race|website=whotv.com|date=November 7, 2018}}</ref> She won nearly the entire state west of Des Moines.<ref>{{cite web |date=November 7, 2018 |title=Iowa Governor Election Results 2018: Live Midterm Map by County & Analysis |url=https://www.politico.com/election-results/2018/iowa/governor/ |website=politico.com}}</ref> In particular, she dominated the state's [[Iowa's 4th congressional district|4th congressional district]], which she carried with 59% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web |author1=J. Miles Coleman |date=July 30, 2020 |title=House Primaries: A Little More Action This Year Than Usual |url=http://centerforpolitics.org/crystalball/articles/house-primaries-a-little-more-action-this-year-than-usual/ |access-date=August 4, 2020 |website=UVA Center For Politics}}</ref> Reynolds is the first woman elected governor of Iowa.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.kcci.com/article/iowa-voters-officially-elect-first-female-governor/24765255|title=Iowa voters officially elect first female governor|first=Alex|last=Kirkpatrick|date=November 7, 2018|website=KCCI}}</ref>

==== 2022 ====
{{Main|2022 Iowa gubernatorial election}}

Reynolds was reelected to a second full term, defeating [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee Deidre DeJear, 58% to 40%.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.iowapublicradio.org/political-news/2022-10-11/2022-election-candidates-iowa-governor-kim-reynolds-deidre-dejear|title=Election Results: Gov. Kim Reynolds wins reelection|date=November 8, 2022|website=Iowa Public Radio}}</ref>

===First term===
Reynolds's elevation to the governorship created a vacancy in the office of lieutenant governor. Reports indicated that Reynolds's selection of a lieutenant governor could be challenged in the [[Iowa Supreme Court]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://whotv.com/2017/05/24/sources-confirm-adam-gregg-set-to-become-governor-reynolds-new-lt-governor/|title=Sources Confirm Adam Gregg Set to Become Governor Reynolds' New Lt. Governor|date=May 25, 2017|website=whotv.com|access-date=May 25, 2017}}</ref> An opinion from the [[Attorney General of Iowa]] indicated that "an individual promoted from lieutenant governor to governor, as was Reynolds, [did] not have the authority to appoint a new lieutenant governor."<ref name="wfcourier.com">{{Cite news|url=http://wcfcourier.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/reynolds-to-make-gregg-acting-lieutenant-governor/article_0efec597-699e-5132-8483-65695ab2dfcd.html|title=Reynolds to make Gregg 'acting' lieutenant governor|last=Murphy|first=Erin|work=Waterloo Cedar Falls Courier|date=May 25, 2017|access-date=May 25, 2017|language=en}}</ref> On May 25, 2017, Reynolds announced that Iowa Public Defender [[Adam Gregg]] would serve as acting lieutenant governor; to avoid litigation, the Reynolds administration stated that Gregg "[would] not hold the official position of lieutenant governor" and would not succeed Reynolds in the event of her inability to serve as governor.<ref name="wfcourier.com"/>

In 2018, Reynolds proposed cutting $10 million from [[Medicaid]], which cares for eligible low-income adults, children, pregnant women, elderly adults and people with disabilities.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Rodriguez|first=Barbara|title=Republicans question Gov. Reynolds' plan to cut $10 million from Medicaid|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2018/01/21/republicans-question-gov-reynolds-plan-cut-10-million-medicaid/1052041001/|date=January 21, 2018|access-date=February 5, 2021|website=[[Des Moines Register]]|language=en-US}}</ref> In 2020, she proposed a one-cent increase in the [[sales taxes in the United States|state sales tax]] (bringing it to 8 cents), offset by a phased reduction in the [[state income tax]], including a cut in the rate for the top bracket from 9% to 5.5%.<ref name=Gruber-Miller>{{cite news|first=Stephen|last=Gruber-Miller|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2020/01/14/kim-reynolds-condition-of-the-state-sales-tax-increase-water-quality-mental-health-income-tax-cuts/4432530002/|title=Kim Reynolds proposes 1-cent sales tax increase to fund water quality, mental health, income tax cuts|newspaper=[[Des Moines Register]]|date=January 14, 2020}}</ref> Reynolds's proposed restructuring of the state tax code would represent a further reduction in income taxes, going beyond 2018 legislation (passed by Republicans in the state legislature and signed into law by Reynolds) that was the largest income tax cut in Iowa history.<ref name=Gruber-Miller/> Her proposed sales-tax increase, however, was largely opposed by state legislators.<ref>{{cite news|first=Rod|last=Boshart|url=https://www.thegazette.com/subject/news/government/iowa-sales-tax-increase-invest-in-iowa-gov-kim-reynolds-20201211|title=Iowa lawmakers mostly oppose Gov. Reynolds' plan to raise sales tax|newspaper=[[The Gazette (Cedar Rapids)|The Gazette]]|date=December 11, 2020}}</ref>

In 2018, after the [[U.S. Supreme Court]]'s decision in ''[[Obergefell v. Hodges]]'' (2015), Reynolds called [[same-sex marriage]] a "settled" issue and said that she did not consider herself obligated to follow the [[Iowa Republican Party]] platform provision against same-sex marriage.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://eu.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2018/06/12/iowa-kim-reynolds-repeal-gay-same-sex-marriage-republican-party/694882002/|title=Iowa Gov. Kim Reynolds says same-sex marriage issue settled; doesn't have to abide by GOP platform|first=William |last=Petroski|date=June 12, 2018|work=[[Des Moines Register]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.iowapublicradio.org/post/reynolds-cutting-energy-efficiency-it-s-balance|title=Reynolds on Cutting Energy Efficiency: 'It's a Balance'|last=Russell|first=Joyce|work=Iowa Public Radio|date=May 8, 2018|access-date=November 3, 2018|language=en|archive-date=November 3, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181103210702/http://www.iowapublicradio.org/post/reynolds-cutting-energy-efficiency-it-s-balance|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Reynolds has supported some of [[Donald Trump]]'s positions.<ref name=ReynoldsManyBelieve>{{cite web|last=Pitt|first=David|url=https://apnews.com/article/election-2020-joe-biden-iowa-elections-kim-reynolds-d63d5b356919a67c6645fc75c33a2dcd|title=Reynolds decries riot but says many believe vote 'not valid'|work=[[Associated Press]]|date=January 7, 2021}}</ref><ref name=SecondChance/> She blocked two-thirds of requests from Democratic state Attorney General [[Tom Miller (politician)|Tom Miller]] to join multi-state lawsuits challenging [[First presidency of Donald Trump|Trump administration]] policies or to submit [[amicus brief]]s in such suits; among the vetoed requests were proposals to challenge Trump policies related to immigration, asylum, abortion, birth control, environmental deregulation, gun policy, and [[LGBT rights]].<ref>{{cite web|first=David|last=Pitt|url=https://apnews.com/article/legislature-bills-iowa-lawsuits-kim-reynolds-1f4d6f1f886d3aef9e0dcb1009577f30|title=Iowa governor has stopped AG from joining anti-Trump suits|work=[[Associated Press]]|date=August 3, 2020}}</ref> Reynolds blocked Miller from including Iowa in a legal challenge to the Trump administration's repeal of the [[Clean Power Plan]], an [[Barack Obama|Obama]]-era regulation that restricted [[greenhouse gas emissions|emissions of greenhouses gases]] (such as [[carbon emissions|carbon]]) to counteract [[Global warming|climate change]].<ref>{{cite web|first=David|last=Pitt|url=https://apnews.com/article/97608a334cd748bd9c64181ecdf97620|title=Iowa governor stops state from challenging Trump coal rule|work=[[Associated Press]]|date=August 14, 2019}}</ref> In 2018, she acknowledged that [[First Trump tariffs|Trump's trade and tariff policies]] were hurting American farmers (as agriculture exports declined due to tariffs imposed by other nations in retaliation for Trump's tariffs), but then claimed that farmers would ultimately benefit.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.omaha.com/eedition/sunrise/articles/reynolds-hubbell-spar-over-tariffs-sexual-harassment/article_d2337f4c-d2ec-5f52-b178-aab89517f273.html|title=Reynolds, Hubbell spar over tariffs, sexual harassment|work=Omaha.com|access-date=November 3, 2018|language=en|archive-date=January 11, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210111134255/https://omaha.com/eedition/sunrise/articles/reynolds-hubbell-spar-over-tariffs-sexual-harassment/article_d2337f4c-d2ec-5f52-b178-aab89517f273.html|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|first=William|last=Petroski|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2018/04/10/reynolds-says-trump-may-help-iowa-farmers-hurt-trade-tariffs/504000002/|title=Reynolds says Trump may help Iowa farmers hurt by trade tariffs|work=[[Des Moines Register]]|access-date=November 3, 2018|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|first=Donnelle|last=Eller|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/money/agriculture/2018/07/20/iowa-politicians-court-farmers-trump-tariffs-sow-fear-ag-country/782196002/|title=Iowa farmers' angst grows as tariffs mount. But many still back Trump and his allies ... for now|work=[[Des Moines Register]]|date=July 20, 2018|language=en}}</ref> Reynolds made campaign appearances with Trump during the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 presidential campaign]]; in the November election, Trump [[2020 United States presidential election in Iowa|carried Iowa]], but lost nationally to [[Joe Biden]], who won both the [[Electoral College (United States)|electoral vote]] and the national popular vote.<ref name=ReynoldsManyBelieve/> After Trump's loss, Reynolds [[Republican reactions to Donald Trump's claims of 2020 election fraud|did not denounce Trump's false claims of election fraud]] and refused to acknowledge Biden's victory until January 2021, when [[2021 United States Electoral College vote count|Congress formally counted the electoral votes]].<ref name=ReynoldsManyBelieve/> She condemned the [[2021 storming of the United States Capitol|storming of the Capitol]], which disrupted the counting of the electoral votes, but said many people believed the election was "not valid".<ref name=ReynoldsManyBelieve/>

In May 2018, Reynolds signed a bill to revamp Iowa's energy efficiency policies.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/7bba9b1b85f642c59866682aff4e9d6a|title=Reynolds signs bill overhauling energy efficiency policies|work=[[Associated Press]]|date=May 4, 2018}}</ref> Also in May 2018, she signed a "[[fetal heartbeat bill]]", one of the nation's most restrictive abortion bans.<ref name=":02">{{Cite news|url=https://eu.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2018/05/04/abortion-ban-law-iowa-fetal-heartbeat/577443002/|title=The nation's strictest abortion ban is now law. Iowa Gov. Kim Reynolds signs 'fetal heartbeat' bill|work=[[Des Moines Register]]|date=May 4, 2018|access-date=November 3, 2018|language=en}}</ref><ref name=OptsNot>{{cite news|first=David|last=Pitt|url=https://apnews.com/article/42407032e7b74490b409b9c6f5e65033|title=Iowa governor opts not to appeal fetal heartbeat law ruling|work=[[Associated Press]]|date=February 18, 2019}}</ref> In January 2019, an Iowa state judge struck the law down as unconstitutional.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnn.com/2019/01/23/us/iowa-fetal-heartbeat-abortion-unconstitutional/index.html|title=Iowa's 'fetal heartbeat' abortion restriction declared unconstitutional|first1=Eric|last1=Levenson|first2=Marlena|last2=Baldacci|website=[[CNN]]|date=January 23, 2019}}</ref> Reynolds chose not to appeal, saying she did not believe that "a losing legal battle" would advance the [[anti-abortion]] cause.<ref name=OptsNot/> She has repeatedly called for an amendment to the state constitution to the effect that it does not protect [[abortion rights]]; such an amendment would overturn a 2019 [[Iowa Supreme Court]] decision concluding that the state constitution does protect the right to an abortion.<ref name=Gruber-Miller/> In 2021, Reynolds signed into law a bill that required women getting abortion to wait for 24 hours; an Iowa court struck the law down.<ref>{{Cite web|first1=William|last1=Morris|first2=Stephen|last2=Gruber-Miller|title=Iowa abortion law requiring 24-hour waiting period permanently blocked by district court|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/crime-and-courts/2021/06/22/iowa-court-permanently-blocks-law-abortion-restriction-24-hour-waiting-period/5305613001/|date=June 22, 2021|access-date=June 23, 2021|website=[[Des Moines Register]]|language=en-US}}</ref>

===Second term===
Reynolds began her first full term on January 18, 2019. In March 2019, she signed into law a bill requiring public universities to protect all speech on campus.<ref>{{cite news | last1=Gruber-Miller | first1=Stephen | last2=Breaux | first2=Aimee | title=Kim Reynolds signs bill requiring Iowa universities to respect 'free speech' on campus | url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2019/03/27/free-speech-on-campus-governor-kim-reynolds-bill-university-iowa-business-leaders-christ-ui-isu-uni/3288307002/ | date=March 27, 2019 | newspaper=[[The Des Moines Register]] | access-date=March 28, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Iowa governor signs campus free speech legislation | url=https://apnews.com/c20185917f3a4a5a8553f2751920a024 | date=March 28, 2019 | work=[[Associated Press]] | access-date=March 28, 2019}}</ref> Through her judicial appointments, Reynolds shifted the Iowa Supreme Court to the [[Right-wing politics|right]].<ref>{{cite web | last1=Pitt | first1=David | title=Iowa Supreme Court takes a right turn under Gov. Reynolds | url=https://www.apnews.com/357e55b3c4ca4432b8a460521f9034ff | date=July 3, 2019 | publisher=[[Associated Press]] | access-date=July 3, 2019}}</ref> Her attorney, Sam Langholz, was appointed to a position in the attorney general's office to defend her policies in court.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/biden-cabinet-iowa-health-tom-miller-abortion-fcf08739117948724380c900414b23be|title=Lawyer hired by Democratic AG defends Iowa's GOP governor|website=[[Associated Press]] |date=April 20, 2021}}</ref>

In December 2019, Reynolds launched an anti-vaping social media campaign in an effort to reduce vaping among Iowa youth.<ref>[https://apnews.com/article/2cc4ba452453da27353d9247577cae20 Iowa governor launches anti-vaping social media campaign], ''[[Associated Press]]'',  (December 11, 2019).</ref> In July 2020, she signed legislation that raised the minimum legal age to buy [[tobacco products]], including vaping products, to 21.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wowt.com/2020/07/07/iowa-gov-reynolds-signs-bill-to-raise-tobacco-usage-age/|title=Iowa Gov. Reynolds signs bill to raise tobacco usage age|work=WOWT|date=July 7, 2020|first=Harper|last=Lundgren}}</ref>

From 2017 to April 2020, Reynolds [[Felony disenfranchisement in the United States|restored the voting rights]] of 543 felons, more than the roughly 200 restorations that her predecessor gave over almost seven years in office.<ref name=SecondChance/> In August 2020, she signed an executive order permitting felons to vote in Iowa elections upon completing their sentence. Iowa previously imposed a lifetime ban on felons voting unless the governor personally restored their voting rights, the strictest law in the country.<ref name=SecondChance/><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.weareiowa.com/article/news/local/local-politics/iowa-governor-kim-reynolds-felon-voting-rights-executive-order-politics-state-capitol/524-5874d3da-ce6c-4a89-859d-a61fc4858b59|title=Iowa no longer lone state with lifetime ban barring felons from voting|website=WOI-DT|date=August 5, 2020 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnn.com/2020/08/05/politics/iowa-governor-ex-felons-voting-rights/index.html|title=Iowa governor signs executive order restoring some ex-felons' voting rights|first=Veronica|last=Stracqualursi|website=[[CNN]]|date=August 5, 2020}}</ref> Explaining her order, Reynolds referred to her experiences two decades earlier, when she twice pleaded guilty to [[DUI]] and subsequently recovered from [[alcoholism]], an experience she cites as an important turning point in her life.<ref name=SecondChance/>

Reynolds has a close relationship with the Iowa [[pig farming|pork industry]], and in particular with [[Iowa Select Farms]], one of the country's largest pork producers. She donated an afternoon of her time as part of a 2019 charity auction to benefit the company's owners' foundation; the owners had contributed almost $300,000 to Reynolds's campaigns.<ref name=PorkBaron>Ryan J. Foley, [https://apnews.com/article/des-moines-iowa-city-coronavirus-pandemic-campaigns-kim-reynolds-1cf07b143b8f528d68dbaf9a8ec4ec19 Iowa governor auctioned off access for pork barons' charity], ''[[Associated Press]]'',  (February 8, 2021).</ref> A Republican donor who is influential in the pork industry placed the winning bid. The director of the Iowa Ethics and Campaign Disclosure Board said that he did not believe the auction violated state law, but attorneys for two former Democratic governors of Iowa said that it created the [[appearance of impropriety]] and was an error in judgment.<ref name=PorkBaron/> In May and July 2020, Reynolds's administration arranged for COVID-19 testing to be done at Iowa Select's [[West Des Moines]] headquarters and at the [[Waverly, Iowa|Waverly]] facility partly owned by another campaign donor, at a time when those most vulnerable to the disease (healthcare workers and residents of [[nursing home]]s and other congregate-living facilities) were unable to timely get tested.<ref name=IowaArrange>David Pitt & Ryan J. Foley, [https://apnews.com/article/public-health-iowa-kim-reynolds-des-moines-coronavirus-pandemic-639cc1c485701ce6eef074a34577d8e2 Iowa arranged COVID-19 tests at office of governor's donors], ''[[Associated Press]]'',  (January 27, 2021).</ref> A separate pork production company that had donated $25,000 to Reynolds's campaign received a disproportionate benefit from a state pandemic business-aid program, receiving 72% of the program's initial rounds of disbursements.<ref>Ryan J. Foley, [https://apnews.com/article/business-iowa-iowa-city-coronavirus-pandemic-campaigns-e97f37fa3274b2afd5ff71c05a4a2a24 Pork exec gives $25K to Iowa governor; company got virus aid], ''[[Associated Press]]'',  (January 20, 2021).</ref> After the testing came to light in January 2021, Polk County Supervisor [[Matt McCoy (politician)|Matt McCoy]] criticized Reynolds, and [[Iowa State Auditor|State Auditor]] [[Rob Sand]] began an investigation into whether special treatment was accorded to political donors over [[essential worker]]s and vulnerable persons.<ref name=IowaArrange/> In 2023, Reynolds signed legislation to strip the state auditor's powers.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-06-02 |title=Reynolds signs law to limit Iowa state auditor's powers |url=https://www.iowapublicradio.org/state-government-news/2023-06-01/reynolds-signs-law-to-limit-iowa-state-auditors-powers |website=Iowa Public Radio |language=en}}</ref> In 2025, she joined a letter opposing [[2018 California Proposition 12]] and supporting the Save Our Bacon Act ({{USBill|119|hr|4673}}).<ref>{{cite press release |title=Gov. Reynolds pens joint letter to Congress stressing protections for pork producers {{!}} Governor Kim Reynolds |url=https://governor.iowa.gov/press-release/2025-07-23/gov-reynolds-pens-joint-letter-congress-stressing-protections-pork-producers |website=governor.iowa.gov |language=en |date=23 July 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Barton |first1=Tom |title=Reynolds, Iowa pork producers want Congress to intervene on California 'bacon ban' |url=https://www.thegazette.com/federal-government/gov-reynolds-iowa-pork-producers-want-congress-to-intervene-on-california-bacon-ban/ |work=[[The Gazette (Cedar Rapids, Iowa)]] |date=23 July 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250725134856/https://www.thegazette.com/federal-government/gov-reynolds-iowa-pork-producers-want-congress-to-intervene-on-california-bacon-ban/ |archive-date=25 July 2025}}</ref>

In March 2021, Reynolds signed into law a bill that shortened the hours of polling places on Election Day, reduced the [[Early voting#United States|early voting]] period, and required that absentee ballots be received by ballot places before the end of Election Day.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web|author=Paul LeBlanc|title=Iowa governor signs controversial law shortening early and Election Day voting|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/03/08/politics/kim-reynolds-voting-iowa/index.html|access-date=2021-03-09|website=CNN|date=March 8, 2021 }}</ref> She said the legislation would protect election integrity.<ref name=":4" /> It was part of a [[Republican efforts to make voting laws more restrictive following the 2020 presidential election|wider effort by Republicans across the country to roll back voting access]]. Democrats won the 2020 presidential election, with Trump and many other Republicans making false claims of fraud.<ref name=":4" />

On April 2, 2021, Reynolds signed a bill allowing individuals to purchase and carry handguns without a permit, a policy known as [[constitutional carry]].<ref>{{cite web | last1=Gruber-Miller | first1= Stephen| title=Iowa Gov. Kim Reynolds signs law allowing permitless handgun carry, purchase | url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2021/04/02/iowa-kim-reynolds-signs-law-allowing-no-permit-carry-purchase-handguns-conceal-carry-constitutional/6965689002/ | date=April 2, 2021 | work=[[The Des Moines Register]] | access-date=April 4, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | last1=Pitt | first1=David | title=Reynolds signs gun bill easing background checks, permits | url=https://apnews.com/article/iowa-kim-reynolds-gun-politics-bills-4c831261c6161f3dad910b0882c07e7e | date=April 2, 2021 | work=[[Associated Press]] | access-date=April 4, 2021}}</ref> Later that month, she signed legislation that would allow landlords to reject tenants who pay rent with [[Section 8 (housing)|Section 8]] vouchers.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Castronuovo|first=Celine|date=2021-05-01|title=Iowa governor signs law allowing landlords to refuse Section 8 vouchers|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/551315-iowa-governor-signs-law-allowing-landlords-to-refuse-section-8-vouchers|access-date=2021-05-03|website=TheHill|language=en}}</ref>

Reynolds signed SF 542 to loosen [[Child labor laws in the United States|child labor laws]]<ref>Multiples sources:
* {{cite news |last1=Opsahl |first1=Robin |title=Reynolds signs law loosening Iowa’s child labor restrictions |url=https://iowacapitaldispatch.com/2023/05/26/reynolds-signs-law-loosening-iowas-child-labor-restrictions/ |work=[[Iowa Capital Dispatch]] |date=May 26, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230601154834/https://iowacapitaldispatch.com/2023/05/26/reynolds-signs-law-loosening-iowas-child-labor-restrictions/ |archive-date=June 1, 2023 |access-date=February 4, 2026 |url-status=live }}
* {{cite web |last1=Sherer |first1=Jennifer |last2=Mast |first2=Nina |title=Iowa governor signs one of the most dangerous rollbacks of child labor laws in the country: 14 states have now introduced bills putting children at risk |url=https://www.epi.org/blog/iowa-governor-signs-one-of-the-most-dangerous-rollbacks-of-child-labor-laws-in-the-country-14-states-have-now-introduced-bills-putting-children-at-risk/ |website=[[Economic Policy Institute]] |date=May 31, 2023 }}
* {{cite news |last1=Akin |first1=Katie |last2=Gruber-Miller |first2=Stephen |title=Gov. Kim Reynolds loosens Iowa child labor laws to let teens work more hours. What to know: |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2023/05/26/iowa-governor-kim-reynolds-signs-child-labor-law-changes-letting-teens-work-longer-hours-more-jobs/70205073007/ |work=[[The Des Moines Register]] |date=May 26, 2023 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20230603215449/https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2023/05/26/iowa-governor-kim-reynolds-signs-child-labor-law-changes-letting-teens-work-longer-hours-more-jobs/70205073007/ |archive-date=June 3, 2023 }}
* {{cite news |last1=Iyer |first1=Kaanita |title=Iowa governor signs bill to loosen child labor laws {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/05/26/politics/iowa-child-labor-law-kim-reynolds/index.html |work=[[CNN]] |date=27 May 2023 |language=en }}
* {{cite news |last1=McFETRIDGE |first1=SCOTT |last2=FINGERHUT |first2=HANNAH |title=Iowa governor signs bill loosening child labor laws |url=https://apnews.com/article/iowa-governor-child-labor-laws-e8cb29e2a45b956489c4a192b7ddeba5 |work=[[AP News]] |date=26 May 2023 |language=en }}</ref> and sent the U.S. Secretary of Labor a letter that said: "I am writing on behalf of small businesses across Iowa that are facing excessive fines due to the U.S. Department of Labor's enforcement of youth labor laws."<ref>{{cite press release |title=Gov. Reynolds Issues Letter to Dept. Of Labor on Excessive Fines Target Iowa Businesses {{!}} Governor Kim Reynolds |url=https://governor.iowa.gov/press-release/2024-07-01/gov-reynolds-issues-letter-dept-labor-excessive-fines-target-iowa-businesses |website=governor.iowa.gov |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240730154434/https://governor.iowa.gov/press-release/2024-07-01/gov-reynolds-issues-letter-dept-labor-excessive-fines-target-iowa-businesses |archive-date=July 30, 2024 |language=en |date=1 July 2024 |access-date=February 4, 2026 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Hardy |first1=Kevin |title=Steep fines in Iowa set off state-federal showdown over child labor laws • Stateline |url=https://stateline.org/2024/07/23/steep-fines-in-iowa-set-off-state-federal-showdown-over-child-labor-laws/ |work=[[Stateline (news)]] |date=23 July 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Baskins |first1=Kevin |title=Federal child labor crackdown in Iowa draws protest from Gov. Kim Reynolds, lawmakers |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/money/business/2024/07/01/federal-child-labor-regulations-crackdown-gets-pushback-from-iowa-congress-members-kim-reynolds/74268188007/ |work=[[The Des Moines Register]] |date=July 1, 2024 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20240703141156/https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/money/business/2024/07/01/federal-child-labor-regulations-crackdown-gets-pushback-from-iowa-congress-members-kim-reynolds/74268188007/ |archive-date=July 3, 2024}}</ref> In 2025, she signed HF 889, enacting [[paid family leave]] for state employees.<ref>{{cite press release |title=Gov. Reynolds signs list of bills into law {{!}} Governor Kim Reynolds |url=https://governor.iowa.gov/press-release/2025-05-27/gov-reynolds-signs-list-bills-law |website=governor.iowa.gov |language=en |date=27 May 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Murphy |first1=Erin |title=Paid family leave for Iowa state workers among bills signed into law by Gov. Reynolds |url=https://www.thegazette.com/state-government/paid-family-leave-for-iowa-state-workers-among-bills-signed-into-law-by-gov-reynolds/ |work=[[The Gazette (Cedar Rapids, Iowa)]] |date=May 27, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250723231301/https://www.thegazette.com/state-government/paid-family-leave-for-iowa-state-workers-among-bills-signed-into-law-by-gov-reynolds/ |archive-date=July 23, 2025 |access-date=February 4, 2026 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== COVID-19 pandemic ====
{{Further|COVID-19 pandemic in Iowa}}

During the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], Reynolds opposed [[Face masks during the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States#Enforcement and challenges|face mask mandates]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite news|last1=Gabriel|first1=Trip|last2=Herndon|first2=Astead W.|date=2020-10-23|title=As Governor Resists Mask Mandate, Iowans Sour on the G.O.P.|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/10/23/us/politics/iowa-kim-reynolds-face-masks.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201023185053/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/10/23/us/politics/iowa-kim-reynolds-face-masks.html |archive-date=2020-10-23 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|access-date=2020-10-25|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> She signed a Proclamation of Disaster Emergency on March 9, 2020.<ref>{{cite web |title=Iowa Gov. Kim Reynolds provides update on COVID-19 cases |url=https://www.ketv.com/article/armed-robbers-steal-dog-while-holding-owner-at-gunpoint/33644598 |website=KETV Omaha |date=March 10, 2020}}</ref> Also that month, she ordered closures of some recreational businesses and additional school closures<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|last=Pitt|first=David|title=Muscatine face mask mandate not valid, Gov. Kim Reynolds says|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2020/07/08/covid-19-iowa-muscatine-face-mask-order-not-valid-gov-kim-reynolds-says/5398087002/|access-date=2020-08-20|website=Des Moines Register|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Rodriguez |first1=Barbara |title=Gov. Kim Reynolds prohibits gatherings of more than 10 people, limits restaurants, closes theaters, casinos, bars |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/health/2020/03/17/coronavirus-iowa-reynolds-state-public-health-emergency-covid-19-social-distancing/5067571002/ |website=Des Moines Register |date=March 18, 2020}}</ref> and ordered a halt to "non-essential" or elective surgeries, including surgical abortions,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/health/2020/03/27/coronavirus-in-iowa-surgical-abortion-suspended-kim-reynolds-non-essential-surgery-proclamation/2930439001/|title=Governor's office says order suspending 'non-essential' surgery includes halting surgical abortions|last=Rodriguez|first=Barbara|website=Des Moines Register|language=en|access-date=2020-03-28}}</ref> but implemented no stay-at-home orders.<ref name=":0" /> In April 2020, Iowa health officials advised Reynolds to enforce face coverings and not ease restrictions. In response, she took the stance that it was better to reopen the economy and encourage people to be responsible and wear masks.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|author=Michael McAuliff, Julio Ochoa, Jackie Fortiér, Blake Farmer|date=October 2, 2020|title=Wear a Mask. If Only It Were That Simple.|url=https://khn.org/news/mask-wearing-enforcement-not-so-simple/|publisher=WUSF/KPCC/Nashville Public Radio|via=Kaiser Health News|language=en-US}}</ref> Reynolds did not implement a requirement to wear masks in public places until after the November 2020 election, when she ordered the mandatory wearing of masks at large gatherings.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Richardson|first=Nick Coltrain and Ian|title=Gov. Kim Reynolds requires Iowans to wear masks at large gatherings to thwart spread of the coronavirus|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2020/11/10/iowa-gov-kim-reynolds-tuesday-news-conference-coronavirus-covid-update-hospitalizations/6227278002/|access-date=2020-11-11|website=Des Moines Register|language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":0" /> In June, recreational businesses such as bars and restaurants were permitted to fully reopen, which was followed by surges in coronavirus infections.<ref name=":0" /> Reynolds said that the effectiveness of face masks in halting the virus's spread was not settled, although doctors and scientists nearly unanimously held that wearing masks in public substantially reduced viral transmission, and their use was recommended by the [[World Health Organization]] (WHO), [[Centers for Disease Control and Prevention]] (CDC), and Iowa's own Department of Health.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Murphy|first=Erin|title=Gov. Kim Reynolds defends lack of face mask mandate|url=https://www.thegazette.com/subject/news/gov-kim-reynolds-defends-lack-of-face-mask-mandate-20200730|access-date=2020-08-20|website=The Gazette|language=en}}</ref> In the absence of any evidence of her contention, in July 2020, Reynolds said that Iowa localities' mask mandates were "not appropriate" and unlawful,<ref name=":1" /> and she sought to block them, making Iowa one of the only U.S. states lacking any enforceable local or state mask mandates.<ref name=FoleyRequire>{{Cite web|author=Ryan J. Foley|url=https://apnews.com/article/146dbfdb47167b0b824f5b44ec8f0697|title=Defying governor, Iowa City mayor mandates masks in public|work=Associated Press|date=July 21, 2020}}</ref> [[Iowa City, Iowa|Iowa City]]'s mayor rejected Reynolds's claim and issued a municipal order requiring the use of face coverings in indoor public places. He cited the state constitution's [[Home rule in the United States|home-rule authority]] and a state statute that grants mayors powers during a time of "emergency or public danger".<ref name=FoleyRequire/>

In April 2020, on the advice of native Iowan actor and entrepreneur [[Ashton Kutcher]], Reynolds signed a $26 million, no-bid contract with Utah startup Nomi Health to develop a [[COVID-19 testing]] program called TestIowa.<ref>Foley, Ryan J. (April 23, 2020). [https://apnews.com/ef5038c7d6e9e08601d469d1a72bba6b "Iowa governor: Tip from Ashton Kutcher led to testing deal."], ''[[Associated Press]].'' Retrieved April 24, 2020.</ref><ref>Henderson, O. Kay (April 23, 2020). [https://www.radioiowa.com/2020/04/23/ashton-kutcher-linked-iowas-governor-to-utah-covid-19-testing-program/ "Ashton Kutcher linked Iowa’s governor to Utah COVID-19 testing program."] Radio Iowa. Retrieved April 24, 2020.</ref><ref>Leys, Tony (April 23, 2020). [https://www.thehawkeye.com/news/20200423/how-tip-from-actor-ashton-kutcher-led-gov-kim-reynolds-to-hire-firm-for-testing-program "How a tip from actor Ashton Kutcher led Gov. Kim Reynolds to hire firm for testing program."] ''[[The Hawk Eye]].'' Retrieved April 24, 2020.</ref> She was eventually sued for refusing to release public records related to the program, which was plagued with errors.<ref>[https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2021/08/19/gov-kim-reynolds-administration-sued-over-test-iowa-records-request/8197907002/ Utah publisher sues Gov. Kim Reynolds for records detailing COVID testing program], ''[[Associated Press]]'', Ryan J. Foley, August 19, 2021. Retrieved March 1, 2022.</ref>

In July 2020, Reynolds said she would invalidate some [[school districts in Iowa|school districts]]' plans to limit in-person classes to one day a week for most students, with online learning on other days, overriding local school districts and requiring students to spend at least half of their schooling in classrooms.<ref name=PittOverride>David Pitt, [https://apnews.com/article/ecc4a3f87122f943f03fe07e37b2bf1b Iowa governor overrides schools, requires in-person classes], ''[[Associated Press]]'',  (July 17, 2020).</ref> The state teachers' union, the Iowa State Education Association, criticized her move.<ref name=PittOverride/><ref name=MandatingFull>David Pitt, [https://apnews.com/article/legislature-des-moines-coronavirus-pandemic-kim-reynolds-iowa-37060af6760216a4182fd47e1bbd24d7 Reynolds signs bill mandating full-time classroom option], ''[[Associated Press]],'' (January 29, 2021).</ref> Iowa public school teachers began sending Reynolds their obituaries in protest.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kcci.com/article/teachers-send-obituaries-to-gov-reynolds-following-return-to-learn-proclamation/33371284|title=Teachers send obituaries to Gov. Reynolds following 'Return to Learn' proclamation|first=Tisia|last=Muzinga|date=July 20, 2020|website=KCCI}}</ref> Despite the outcry from teachers, who noted that [[social distancing]] was often impossible in school settings and that many teachers had not yet been vaccinated, in late January 2021 Reynolds signed a bill, passed by the state legislature along party lines, that required school districts to provide full-time in-person classes upon parents' request.<ref name=MandatingFull/>

In 2020, Reynolds had the lowest approval rating of any governor in the nation for her handling of the COVID-19 pandemic, with an approval rating of 28% in July<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.covidstates.org/reports/executive-approval-update-7 |title= The State of the Nation: A 50-State COVID-19 Survey Report #6: Update on the Approval of Executive Performance During COVID-19 |last=Baum |first=Matthew A. |display-authors=etal |date= July 29, 2020 |version= 2 |doi=10.31219/osf.io/ymskh |s2cid= 262481206 |access-date= February 29, 2024}}</ref> and 26% in September.<ref>Andrew Pearce, [https://kwwl.com/2020/09/16/at-26-gov-reynolds-still-has-the-lowest-approval-rating-in-the-nation-for-handling-of-pandemic/ At 26%, Gov. Reynolds still has the lowest approval rating in the nation for handling of pandemic], KWWL (September 16, 2020).</ref> COVID-19 in Iowa peaked in November 2020, but remained high into the next year. In late January 2021, the state had the nation's third-highest positivity rate<ref name=MandatingFull/> and third-lowest per capita vaccination rate.<ref>David Pitt, [https://apnews.com/article/health-kim-reynolds-iowa-coronavirus-pandemic-9176b2d57df780a0a027d811834e5336 Iowa's vaccination rate 3rd-lowest in US; 5,000 virus deaths], ''[[Associated Press]]'',   (February 4, 2021).</ref>

In September 2020, the Reynolds administration broke with the CDC's quarantine guidelines, revoking guidelines recommending that people exposed to the virus quarantine for 14 days, "if the infected person and close contacts were wearing masks properly".<ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-09-30|title=Iowa Relaxes COVID-19 Quarantine Rules, Breaking With CDC Recommendation|url=https://www.iowapublicradio.org/health/2020-09-29/iowa-relaxes-covid-19-quarantine-rules-breaking-with-cdc-recommendation|access-date=2020-10-03|website=Iowa Public Radio|language=en}}</ref> In October 2020, she appeared at an indoor fundraiser for the Republican Party and a large rally in a hangar for President Trump; attendees did not wear masks or socially distance at the events.<ref name=":1" />

In November 2020, Reynolds issued a statewide mask mandate, ordering anyone over age 2 to wear a mask in indoor public spaces, a reversal of her previous claim that face masks are ineffective. When issuing the order, Reynolds said, "No one wants to do this. I don't want to do this", adding, "If Iowans don't buy into this, we'll lose. Businesses will close once again, more schools will be forced to go online, and our health care system will fail."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.npr.org/sections/coronavirus-live-updates/2020/11/17/935648739/iowa-governor-reverses-course-issues-mask-mandate-as-covid-19-cases-rise|title=Iowa Governor Reverses Course, Issues Mask Mandate As COVID-19 Cases Rise|last=Neuman|first=Scott|date=November 17, 2020|work=NPR|access-date=17 November 2020}}</ref>

In February 2021, Reynolds rolled back mask requirements in indoor public places and restrictions on indoor dining.<ref>{{Cite news|author=Dustin Jones|date=February 2, 2021|title=Iowa Rolls Back Coronavirus Restrictions|publisher=NPR|url=https://www.npr.org/2021/02/05/964689123/iowa-rolls-back-coronavirus-restrictions|access-date=}}</ref> She did so without consulting with experts at the [[Iowa Department of Public Health]].<ref name=":3">{{Cite web|last=Coltrain|first=Nick|title='We're dialing down, we're opening up': Gov. Kim Reynolds explains why she dropped mask rules|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2021/02/08/iowa-covid-mask-mandate-reynolds-dropped-without-public-health-input-democrats-say/4437633001/|access-date=2021-02-10|website=Des Moines Register|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>David Pitt, [https://apnews.com/article/pandemics-public-health-iowa-health-coronavirus-pandemic-2662c418b50f38e8900ee95f68f5483f Iowa governor lifts mask mandate without public health input], ''[[Associated Press]]'', (February 8, 2021).</ref> The CDC had urged states not to loosen their COVID-19 public health measures.<ref name=":3" />

In March 2021, Reynolds voiced her opposition to the [[American Rescue Plan Act of 2021]], saying it would "provide bigger checks to states who chose aggressive shutdowns and mismanaged their state budgets." In September 2021, she touted $100 million in funding for Iowa's water infrastructure that was funded by the bill.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Cunning|first=Zach|title=Kim Reynolds, Iowa governor who touted American Rescue Plan funds many times, set to give GOP response to State of the Union|url=https://heartlandsignal.com/2022/03/01/kim-reynolds-iowa-governor-who-touted-american-rescue-plan-funds-many-times-set-to-give-gop-response-to-state-of-the-union/|access-date=2022-03-03|website=Heartland Signal|date=March 2022 |language=en-US}}</ref>

In May 2021, Reynolds signed into law a bill that prohibited businesses and local governments from requiring customers to have [[proof of vaccination]].<ref name=":5">{{Cite web|last=Richardson|first=Ian|title=Gov. Kim Reynolds signs law limiting use of 'vaccine passports' in Iowa|url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2021/05/20/iowa-gov-kim-reynolds-vaccine-passports-law-limiting-use-prove-covid-vaccination/5074442001/|access-date=2021-08-22|website=Des Moines Register|language=en-US}}</ref> She also signed into law a bill that prohibited school districts from requiring masks.<ref name=":5" />

===Third term===
On March 1, 2022, Reynolds was selected to deliver the [[Response to the State of the Union address|Republican response to]] [[President of the United States|President]] [[Joe Biden|Biden]]'s State of the Union address.<ref>{{Cite web |author=Clare Foran |title=Iowa Gov. Kim Reynolds set to give GOP response to Biden's State of the Union address |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/03/01/politics/republicans-state-of-the-union-response/index.html |access-date=2022-03-02 |website=CNN|date=March 2022 }}</ref> On March 3, 2022, she signed into law a bill that bans [[Transgender people in sports|transgender girls and women]] from participating on designated female sports teams.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Petersen |first1=Abbie |title=Iowa Governor Kim Reynolds signs transgender sports bill into law |url=https://www.ketv.com/article/iowa-governor-kim-reynolds-signs-transgender-sports-bill-into-law/39318826 |access-date=5 March 2022 |work=KETV |date=Mar 3, 2022}}</ref> In January 2023, Reynolds signed the Students First Act, which made private school vouchers available in Iowa for the first time.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ruby F. McAllister |date=2024-01-18 |title=New K-8 private Christian school aims to start classes in Tama Co. in 2025 |url=https://www.northtamatelegraph.com/news/education/2024/01/18/new-k-8-private-christian-school-aims-to-start-classes-in-tama-co-in-2025/ |access-date=2024-02-29 |language=en-US}}</ref> On March 22, 2023, she signed a law banning the prescription of [[puberty blocker]]s, [[Transgender hormone therapy|cross-sex hormones]], and [[gender-affirming surgery]] to minors, as well as a law prohibiting people from using school restrooms that do not align with their [[Sex assignment|sex at birth]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Akin |first=Katie |date=March 22, 2023 |title=Gender-affirming care ban for kids, trans bathroom bill are now Iowa law. What they do |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2023/03/22/gender-affirming-care-ban-and-transgender-bathroom-bill-become-iowa-law-what-it-means/70004084007/ |access-date=May 20, 2024 |work=[[Des Moines Register]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=March 23, 2023 |title=Iowa governor signs gender-affirming care ban, bathroom law |url=https://apnews.com/article/reynolds-iowa-transgender-ban-bathroom-e1651a8785586274f66819dad28b471e |access-date=May 20, 2024 |work=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref>

In November 2023, Reynolds endorsed [[Ron DeSantis]] for the [[2024 Republican Party presidential primaries]], saying she did not believe Trump could win the general election.<ref>{{Cite news |date=November 6, 2023 |title= Iowa Gov. Kim Reynolds says Trump can't win in 2024 as she endorses Ron DeSantis |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/iowa-gov-kim-reynolds-says-trump-cant-win-2024-endorses-ron-desantis-rcna123788| work = NBC News }}</ref> She said she would endorse the Republican nominee and endorsed Trump in March 2024.<ref>{{Cite news |date=March 7, 2024 |title= Kim Reynolds, Joni Ernst throw support behind Donald Trump's presidential campaign |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/elections/endorsement/2024/03/07/iowa-governor-kim-reynolds-senator-joni-ernst-endorse-donald-trump-for-president-over-joe-biden-2024/72882340007/| work = Des Moines Register}}</ref> Around this time, a survey found that Reynolds had the lowest approval rating of any U.S. governor.<ref>{{Cite web |title=West Virginia Sen. Joe Manchin Remains One of America's Least Popular Officials |url=https://pro.morningconsult.com/analysis/senator-governor-rankings-joe-manchin |access-date=2024-08-12 |website=Morning Consult Pro |language=en}}</ref>

In 2024, Reynolds signed a bill allowing state law enforcement to arrest undocumented migrants if they had previously been deported from or denied admission to the United States. The bill also enables state courts to issue deportation orders for such individuals.<ref>{{Cite news  |date=April 10, 2024 |title= Iowa governor signs bill that lets state arrest and deport some migrants |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2024/04/10/iowa-governor-signs-bill-that-lets-state-arrest-and-deport-some-migrants-00151654| work = Politico }}</ref> On February 1, 2024, she introduced a bill defining "man" and "woman" based on [[Human reproductive system|reproductive anatomy]] and requiring that official identification documents list sex at birth.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Pfannenstiel |first1=Brianne |last2=Galen |first2=Bacharier |date=February 5, 2024 |title=Kim Reynolds introduces bill defining 'man' and 'woman'; opponents brand it 'LGBTQ erasure' |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2024/02/01/iowa-governor-kim-reynolds-introduces-bill-defining-man-woman-criticized-lgbtq-erasure/72441900007/ |access-date=May 20, 2024 |work=[[Des Moines Register]]}}</ref>

On February 28, 2025, Reynolds signed Senate File 418, which eliminated [[gender identity]] as a protected class from the Iowa Civil Rights Act.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://governor.iowa.gov/press-release/2025-02-28/gov-reynolds-releases-statement-signing-sf-418?form=MG0AV3 |title=Gov. Reynolds releases statement on signing SF 418|website=governor.iowa.gov|date=February 28, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Trautmann |first=Mike |title=Gov. Kim Reynolds explains why she signed the Iowa law repealing transgender civil rights |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2025/02/28/kim-reynolds-why-she-signed-iowa-trans-bill-civil-rights-act/80867304007/ |access-date=2025-02-28 |website=The Des Moines Register |language=en-US}}</ref> This made Iowa the first U.S. state to remove gender identity as a protected class from a civil rights law.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lim |first=Clarissa-Jan |date=2025-02-28 |title=Iowa becomes first state to remove civil rights protections for gender identity |url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/politics/iowa-becomes-first-state-to-remove-civil-rights-protections-for-gender-identity/ar-AA1A0Ixz?ocid=BingNewsSerp}}</ref>

On April 11, 2025, Reynolds announced she will not seek [[2026 Iowa gubernatorial election|reelection in 2026]].<ref>{{cite news|url= https://apnews.com/article/iowa-governor-kim-reynolds-84052fdcc9fdca605b15dc256e0b30ff|title=Iowa Republican Gov. Kim Reynolds announces she won’t seek reelection in 2026|newspaper=Associated Press|last=Fingerhut|first=Hannah|date=April 11, 2025|access-date=April 11, 2025}}</ref>

==Personal life==
[[File:Kim Reynolds and Kevin Reynolds in 2010.jpg|230px|thumb|Reynolds with husband Kevin Reynolds in 2010]]
Kim Reynolds married Kevin Reynolds in 1982.<ref name= "CDiag"/> They have three children.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nonpareilonline.com/news/local/reynolds-set-to-become-iowa-s-first-female-governor/article_3896b06d-3bc6-5341-a72f-9062d93618e8.html|title=Reynolds set to become Iowa's first female governor|publisher=The Daily Nonpareil|last=Leu|first=Jon|date=December 8, 2016|access-date=March 29, 2018}}</ref> 

Kevin Reynolds was diagnosed with lung cancer in September 2023.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Atkin |first1=Katie |title=Teary Kim Reynolds updates First Gentleman Kevin Reynolds' lung cancer treatment |url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2023/10/25/gov-kim-reynolds-husband-doing-well-after-lung-cancer-diagnosis/71315867007/ |website=Des Moines Register |access-date=18 December 2023}}</ref> His cancer went into remission, but in October 2026 there was a recurrence of the cancer.<ref name= "CDiag">{{citeweb|url=https://iowacapitaldispatch.com/2026/10/19/governors-husband-kevin-reynolds-diagnosed-with-cancer-recurrence/|title=Governor’s husband, Kevin Reynolds, diagnosed with cancer recurrence|date=October 19, 2026|access-date=October 25, 2026|publisher=[[Iowa Capital Dispatch]]}}</ref> He also had a tumor removed from his brain.<ref name= "CDiag"/>

Reynolds attends the Lutheran Church of Hope.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2020/04/29/kim-reynolds-says-her-church-still-meet-online-despite-lifted-coronavirus-covid-19-restrictions/3048532001/ | title=Gov. Kim Reynolds says Iowans can go back to church. Hers won't be among those meeting in-person | website=The Des Moines Register}}</ref>

Reynolds was twice charged with [[driving under the influence of alcohol]], in 1999 and in August 2000.<ref name=SecondChance>{{Cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/9a546f80c52fbec551d8dc70aca7d270|title=Iowa governor got 2nd chance; she thinks felons should, too|date=April 1, 2020|website=AP News|last=Pitt |first=David}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/news/politics/2017/01/12/kim-reynolds-iowa-incoming-governor-alcohol-addiction-example/96486688/|title=Incoming governor wants to be an example for those with alcohol addiction|newspaper=Des Moines Register|last=Young|first=Aaron|date=January 12, 2017|access-date=June 29, 2017}}</ref> In 2000, she was initially charged with Second Offense DUI, but was allowed to plead guilty to a misdemeanor.<ref name=SecondChance/> In 2017, Reynolds said she had sought inpatient treatment for alcoholism after her second arrest and had been sober for nearly 17 years.<ref name=SecondChance/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2017/06/29/kim-reynolds-iowa-governor-215318/|title=The Governor of Trump's America|work=Politico Magazine|last=Alberta|first=Tim|date=June 29, 2017}}</ref>

